<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    ===================================================================
    THE FIRST BUILDING BLOCK, AND THE RULE THAT LET IT EXIST
    ===================================================================

    Since Phase 0 the rule has been: a project moves to BuildingBlocks only
    when two or more services ALREADY have the same code, and only when that
    code is purely technical. Phase 4 named the trigger out loud - "when
    Ordering arrives there will be four copies, and four is when we extract".

    There are now four: Catalog, Basket, Inventory and Ordering each validate
    the same tokens with the same forty lines. Extracting on the second copy
    would have been a guess about what would turn out to be common. Extracting
    on the fourth is a measurement.

    WHAT IS IN HERE: the MECHANISM. How to read a bearer token, verify its
    signature, and check issuer, audience and lifetime. Nothing about
    CommerceFlow's business.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN HERE:

      - Role name constants. "Admin" still lives in CatalogRoles,
        InventoryRoles and OrderingRoles, duplicated three times on purpose.
        The MECHANISM (how a token is validated) is shared; the POLICY (which
        roles this service cares about) is not. Basket cares about no roles at
        all - a shared Roles class would force it to depend on a concept it
        does not use, and every new role in the system would mean redeploying
        every service.

      - Any DTO, entity or contract that crosses a service boundary. The moment
        Catalog and Basket share a compiled ProductResponse, they can no longer
        be deployed independently, and the whole architecture reduces to a
        distributed monolith.

    The test to apply before adding anything here: "if this changes, must every
    service be rebuilt and redeployed together?" If the answer is yes AND that
    is acceptable - because it is infrastructure that genuinely moves in
    lockstep - it belongs. If the answer is yes and that is NOT acceptable, it
    does not.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" Version="10.0.12" />

    <!-- ValidateDataAnnotations() lives here rather than in the framework. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.DataAnnotations" Version="10.0.12" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
